		<!--
			Decorative and electronic blocks: the fittings that were silently setting the
			simulation cost of an entire ship.

			A light is a plastic housing around a glass lens and a light source. Falling through to
			DefaultThermodynamics gave it mild steel's 50 W/(m K) on a 16 to 24 kg body, and
			stiffness is conductance over capacity, so the least massive block on a ship ended up
			the stiffest thing on it. A field run measured a 36-strong set of light corners
			demanding 21.4 substeps where the armour, trusses and reactors around them asked for
			five or fewer — and a grid takes as many substeps as its stiffest element needs, with
			everything else paying for all of them.

			The fix is not a fudge: these really are insulators. Plastic is about 0.2 W/(m K) and
			glass about 1, against steel's 50. Giving them figures a materials table would
			recognise drops their demand from 21.4 to about 0.4 and hands the whole grid back to
			its structure, which is what should have been setting the pace all along.

			See docs/field-tuning.md for the measurement.
		-->
		<Definition>
			<Id>
				<TypeId>InteriorLight</TypeId>
				<SubtypeId>DefaultThermodynamics</SubtypeId>
			</Id>
			<ModExtensions>
				<Group Name="ThermalBlockProperties">
					<Bool Name="ExcludeFromSimulation" Value="false" />
					<!--W/(m K), real units: plastic housing and a glass lens-->
					<Decimal Name="Conductivity" Value="2" />
					<!--J/(kg K), real units: glass and polymer-->
					<Decimal Name="SpecificHeat" Value="900" />
					<Decimal Name="Emissivity" Value="0.9" />
					<Decimal Name="ExposedSurfaceMultiplier" Value ="1" />
					<Decimal Name="ProducerWasteEnergy" Value="0" />
					<Decimal Name="ConsumerWasteEnergy" Value="0.9" />
					<Decimal Name="CriticalTemperature" Value="500" />
					<Decimal Name="OverheatDamagePerKelvin" Value="1"/>
				</Group>
			</ModExtensions>
		</Definition>

